    <section class="cv-section">
      <h2 class="cv-h">Research Statement</h2>
      <div class="cv-body cv-prose">
        <p>
          Agent systems are reconstituting, for the first time outside human
          society, the political forms modernity dissolved: sovereignty,
          organic hierarchy, constitutional order. The AI safety community has
          forecasters, interpretability researchers, and alignment engineers.
          It does not yet have a political theory of agent societies. I am
          building one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The programme is formal, and it already has results. Distributed
          shutdown is a sovereignty problem — who decides the exception when
          no principal knows enough to act alone — and it admits
          information-theoretic lower bounds (AAAI 2027, under review).
          Encoding AI goals as constitutional principles inherits
          Gibbard–Satterthwaite impossibility. The largest published AI-race
          simulation shows that reducing hostility <em>raises</em> simulated
          existential risk — Hobbesian selection pressure, measured rather
          than conjectured.
        </p>
        <p>
          I build this theory from inside a frontier lab. At ByteDance Seed I
          own training-data quality for frontier vision-language models — work
          that carried the model to tool-free SOTA alongside the Gemini
          series. Political theories of AI are mostly written far from the
          machinery; frontier engineering mostly runs without political
          theory. My programme stands on both.
        </p>
        <p>
          A second programme runs in parallel: verification-first AI for
          science — multi-model research pipelines in which no claim survives
          on eloquence. Every result must pass mechanical verification, from
          SAT certificates to the Lean 4 kernel, and the pipeline never emits
          <em>solved</em>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

